WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.100
Having the right actor or actress is an integral
aspect of any successful TV show.

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.070
Sometimes, these stars are so important that
the show can't go on without them.

00:00:09.070 --> 00:00:13.340
Here are some major TV shows that just couldn't
recover from the loss and replacement of a

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:14.160
leading actor.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.360
Sleepy Hollow, Fox's supernatural police drama,
was always a little bit different than other

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.060
cop shows.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.560
For starters, one of its main characters was
Ichabod Crane from Washington Irving's short

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.950
story "The Legend of Sleepy Hollow," who comes
back to life in the present day after having

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:30.900
died 200 or so years prior.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:35.200
Crane then helps fight supernatural menaces
alongside police lieutenant Abbie Mills.

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:39.999
Over three seasons, Sleepy Hollow settled
into its role as a quirky police procedural,

00:00:39.999 --> 00:00:44.899
and then it did the unthinkable Abbie died
and actress Nicole Beharie left the series.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:49.260
Despite a steep loss of viewers from season
1 to season 3, Sleepy Hollow returned for

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.820
a fourth season without Abbie.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:54.920
Ichabod was now partnered with a Homeland
Security agent named Diana Thomas.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:57.840
The cast reboot didn't do Sleepy Hollow any
favors, though.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.100
The show fell even further in the ratings
and was soon canceled.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.720
After seven seasons of the hit Fox sitcom,
actor Topher Grace decided to leave That '70s

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.120
Show in order to more fiercely pursue his
blossoming film career.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.159
Eric wasn't the only member of the gang who
left; Kelso, played by the show's other biggest

00:01:14.159 --> 00:01:18.869
star, Ashton Kutcher, also left the show,
although Kutcher stuck around for a few episodes

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:21.770
in season 8 to explain his character's departure.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:27.289
To replace two very big holes in its cast,
producers hired just one actor, Mad TV veteran

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:28.120
Josh Meyers.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.619
His character, Randy, gets a job at Hyde's
record store and has a brief relationship

00:01:32.619 --> 00:01:34.480
with Donna, Eric's old girlfriend.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.829
Neither Randy nor Meyers had much time to
mesh with the ensemble; the decimated cast

00:01:38.829 --> 00:01:42.770
may have contributed to the show's diminished
popularity and inevitable ending.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.530
The eighth season of That '70s Show would
be its last, although Grace and Kutcher would

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.480
both come back to say good-bye for the finale.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.740
"It's like we never run out of things to talk
about."

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:53.960
"I knew you burnouts would be down here.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.640
It's almost midnight, Mrs. Foreman is pouring
the champagne."

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:02.060
Step by Step was an ABC sitcom about Carol
and Frank Lambert, two single parents who

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.440
bring their families together.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:07.520
In addition to all their kids, Frank's nephew
Cody lived in a van outside the house.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:12.160
Cody was a good-natured, air-headed California
dude, basically a sitcom version of Ted, from

00:02:12.161 --> 00:02:13.800
Bill &amp; Ted's Excellent Adventure.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.960
"And I'm Ted Theodore Logan.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:16.740
Yeah!"

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:21.600
Affable and enthusiastic acting by Sasha Mitchell
made the character a fan favorite.

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.800
But in 1995, when Mitchell was arrested for
spousal assault.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:30.020
He was convicted later that year, and sentenced
to three years of probation and spousal abuse

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:30.520
counseling.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.040
Mitchell wound up behind bars for violating
his parole, and reportedly attacked his wife

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:35.540
again.

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:40.080
At the beginning of the sixth season of Step
by Step, Cody was gone, replaced by Jean-Luc,

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.840
a heavily accented hairdresser colleague of
Carol's.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.180
Playing Jean-Luc was Bronson Pinchot, best
known for playing the excitable, heavily-accented

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:52.080
Balki on Perfect Strangers and the excitable,
heavily-accented Serge in Beverly Hills Cop.

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.340
The new cast addition didn't work, and ABC
canceled Step by Step at the end of the season.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:03:00.680
The show then moved to CBS for a final year...
without Pinchot.

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.640
After the first season of The Facts of Life,
the number of main characters was cut from

00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:10.910
10 to just five: Eastland boarding school
housemother Mrs. Garrett and four female students.

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:15.230
After those four graduated in the fourth season,
Mrs. Garrett opened a gourmet shop and hired

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:16.260
her former students.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.310
And after season seven, Mrs. Garrett herself,
Charlotte Rae, left the show.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:24.190
She thought it was time to move on, later
telling Entertainment Weekly that the show

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.660
could continue on without her.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.240
But producers loved the show's dynamic of
a wise older woman doling out advice to young

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.800
women, so they created the role of Mrs. Garrett's
sister Beverly Ann.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.120
However, the replacement didn't quite work,
and by the ninth season, The Facts of Life

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.860
was unceremoniously cancelled.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:45.200
American Idol has created a number of stars,
such as Kelly Clarkson, Jennifer Hudson, Adam

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.240
Lambert, and, of course, Simon Cowell.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.380
Audiences couldn't get enough of the judge,
who was often brutally and hilariously honest

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.180
with contestants.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:53.980
"Okay.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:56.720
Best singer in America.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.900
I can honestly say you are the worst singer
in America."

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.820
Cowell was the biggest star of the series,
which was the most-watched show on TV for

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:06.980
an unprecedented eight straight seasons.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.540
But in 2010, after nine seasons, he moved
on, in part to develop the American version

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.700
of his British talent series The X Factor.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:18.780
Idol producers had a hard time replacing Cowell,
as well as fellow original judge Paula Abdul.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:22.930
The Idol judges' table saw a lot of stars
take a stab at it, but actual singers just

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:26.880
couldn't fill the void left by the music industry
guy who criticizes singers.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:31.310
By the time Fox pulled the plug on American
Idol in 2016, it had slipped out of the top

00:04:31.310 --> 00:04:32.520
20 most-viewed shows.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:34.320
"Maybe we've made a mistake.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.520
I mean, maybe we've made a mistake."

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:42.200
News Radio, NBC's critically-acclaimed but
low-watched comedy, had no plans to replace

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.520
one of its characters and best-known star
but tragedy forced the producers' hand.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.720
Between the show's fourth and fifth season,
star Phil Hartman was shot to death by his

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.460
troubled wife.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.620
It was a horrific crime, and a devastating
loss for the show.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.960
But in order to complete enough episodes to
sell the show into syndication, NewsRadio

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:00.840
was picked up for another season anyway.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.380
In the season premiere, it was explained that
Hartman's character, pompous radio newsreader

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:07.540
Bill McNeal, died of a sudden heart attack.

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.540
Replacing him at the newsreader's desk was
a new character, Max Louis.

00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:15.890
Weird, insecure, and wholly unqualified for
the position, Max was portrayed by Hartman's

00:05:15.890 --> 00:05:18.720
friend and Saturday Night Live costar Jon
Lovitz.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:23.100
While Lovitz is a great comic actor, he just
couldn't overcome the sadness looming over

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:23.600
NewsRadio.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.780
Lovitz's first season would be the show's
last.

00:05:26.780 --> 00:05:30.700
For the Fox television adaptation of Lethal
Weapon, Clayne Crawford took over for Mel

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.880
Gibson as "loose cannon" Martin Riggs.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:37.320
And if on-set reports published by Variety
are any indication, Crawford was even harder

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.680
to control than his character.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:43.710
In an episode he directed, a special effects
explosion went awry, sending a piece of debris

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:45.420
at co-star Damon Wayans.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.160
When Wayans complained, Crawford referred
to his co-star as a "crybaby."

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.480
Another time he reportedly screamed and swore
at a group of children who wandered near;

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:58.020
on yet another occasion, he reportedly proved
so hard to work with that a director walked

00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:59.810
off the set in the middle of a shoot.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:04.170
In May 2018, after two seasons, producers
eliminated what they thought was the root

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.620
cause of the show's problems and fired Crawford.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:10.651
They rushed to find somebody to replace him,
and quickly hired Seann William Scott to play

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.640
Wesley Cole, a new partner for Detective Roger
Murtaugh.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:15.500
Scott couldn't save the show, however.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:19.470
All that tension didn't totally dissipate,
with Wayans announcing that he planned to

00:06:19.470 --> 00:06:21.400
leave the series in the middle of the season.

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:25.980
Fox decided to wash their hands of the whole
thing in May 2019 and canceled Lethal Weapon.

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:32.860
From 1998 to 2007, Kevin James and Leah Remini
starred on the popular CBS sitcom King of

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:33.360
Queens.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:37.860
James would go on to star in a string of movies
like Grown Ups and Paul Blart: Mall Cop, but

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.610
in 2016, he returned to the weekly CBS sitcom
grind with Kevin Can Wait.

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:46.570
James portrayed Kevin Gable, a family man
who has to delay retiring from his police

00:06:46.570 --> 00:06:48.660
job due to family and money issues.

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:51.780
Playing opposite James was Erinn Hayes as
Kevin's wife, Donna Gable.

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:54.040
At least, that's how it was for the first
season.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:58.199
Kevin Can Wait producers revamped the show
for season two: They killed off Donna Gable

00:06:58.199 --> 00:06:59.250
and fired Hayes.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.190
James explained the decision to the New York
Daily News by saying:

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:04.550
"We were literally just running out of ideas."

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:07.860
They also brought in a new face, who was really
a familiar face.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:12.560
Surprisingly, Leah Remini joined Kevin Can
Wait as Kevin's business partner, Vanessa.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:16.510
However, the nostalgic rebooting couldn't
overcome the weird behind-the-scenes shakeup,

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.900
and ratings actually dropped in season two.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:23.620
After the conclusion of the sitcom's second,
ill-fated season, CBS didn't wait to cancel

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:24.120
Kevin.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.620
An ensemble dramedy with a culturally diverse
cast set in an often surreal rural Alaska,

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:33.430
Northern Exposure was radically different
from most other shows on television.

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:38.020
Rob Morrow starred as Dr. Joel Fleischman,
a snobby New York City doctor who heads north

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.040
to service the tiny town of Cicely.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:44.480
Northern Exposure was a big hit, reaching
the top 20 in the ratings and earning four

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:49.680
straight Emmy nominations for Outstanding
Drama Series, and winning the prize in 1992.

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:53.860
But Fleischman's desire to leave Alaska never
faded, and Morrow wanted to bounce, too.

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:57.880
Halfway through the show's sixth season, Morrow
fled Northern Exposure, reportedly to pursue

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:00.520
new acting challenges and a movie career.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:05.860
Replacing him was a new physician: Dr. Phillip
Capra, portrayed by Paul Provenza, a respected

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:09.680
stand-up comedian who had little acting experience
apart from his work on sitcoms.

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:14.260
That inexperience seems to have showed; after
just half a season with Provenza at the helm,

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.169
the network canceled Northern Exposure.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:21.120
The high school sitcom Head of the Class debuted
on ABC in 1986.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:25.620
It explored the relationships between a diverse
group of highly advanced Manhattan students

00:08:25.620 --> 00:08:28.669
and their history teacher Charlie Moore, played
by Howard Hesseman.

00:08:28.669 --> 00:08:32.759
A big part of why Head of the Class worked
is that it was ensemble-focused; the various

00:08:32.759 --> 00:08:37.229
kids had personal and academic problems, and
Mr. Moore helped them sort things out.

00:08:37.229 --> 00:08:41.300
Hesseman portrayed Moore with understatement
and subtlety, letting his younger co-stars

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:42.320
take center stage.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.560
However, the actor hated Head of the Class.

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:48.600
In 1989, he told a reporter that he thought
the show didn't have much worth.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.100
He said:

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.379
"We're not doing the show that I was led to
believe I'd do, and it's difficult for me

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:53.160
to get off that."

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:56.260
Unsurprisingly, Hesseman left the show in
1990.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.640
In his place was Scottish comedian Billy Connolly
as a teacher named Billy McGregor.

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:04.899
All of a sudden, a show about high school
kids' problems was a show about wacky Mr.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:05.400
McGregor.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.080
ABC seemed to realize that, too.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:12.120
In 1991, the network canceled Head of the
Class and quickly ordered up a new show called

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:12.620
Billy.

00:09:12.680 --> 00:09:16.940
Connolly continued to play McGregor, who was
now living as a boarder in a California single

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:17.840
mother's house.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:21.980
Community never had a huge audience during
its five-year run on NBC.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:27.360
Nevertheless, NBC kept renewing the critically-acclaimed
comedy about the disparate people who comprise

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.260
a Colorado community college study group.

00:09:29.629 --> 00:09:34.100
That loyalty remained even after increasing
difficulties with Community creator and showrunner

00:09:34.100 --> 00:09:36.520
Dan Harmon, who was fired, and then rehired.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.570
However, five seasons was ultimately NBC's
limit, leaving internet giant Yahoo! to step

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.040
in and renew Community for a sixth season.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.740
"That show's gonna last 3 weeks."

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:50.960
"Six seasons and a movie!"

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:57.119
Original regulars Joel McHale, Gillian Jacobs,
Danny Pudi, Ken Jeong, and Alison Brie followed

00:09:57.119 --> 00:10:01.119
the series to Yahoo!, but the show had been
losing cast members, and kept losing them

00:10:01.119 --> 00:10:02.740
as it transitioned to streaming.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:07.070
Chevy Chase, Donald Glover, and Yvette Nicole
Brown were not part of the regular cast of

00:10:07.070 --> 00:10:08.649
Community's sixth season.

00:10:08.649 --> 00:10:12.970
New actors were added, but the bandaid casting
just didn't work out, and there was no seventh

00:10:12.970 --> 00:10:14.200
season of Community.

00:10:14.329 --> 00:10:18.700
Not enough people tuned in and the show didn't
generate as much advertising revenue as Yahoo!

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:19.280
had hoped.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.320
In fact, the revenue was so small that it
effectively led to the complete shutdown of

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:23.820
Yahoo!

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:24.320
Screen.

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:25.200
Yahoo!

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.860
CFO Ken Goldman said on an earnings call that
the company lost $42 million because of Community

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.800
and its two other original series.

00:10:33.620 --> 00:10:37.639
Scrubs, a dramedy about medical interns desperately
trying to figure out what they were doing

00:10:37.639 --> 00:10:42.600
as they worked in a hospital, was a critically-acclaimed,
Emmy-nominated hit over its first few seasons

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:43.340
on NBC.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:48.550
The network finally dropped Scrubs after seven
seasons in 2008, but got picked up by ABC

00:10:48.550 --> 00:10:50.670
to finish things off with an eighth season.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.199
The show's writers and cast prepared a true
series finale to cap off those episodes, only

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:58.560
for ABC to unexpectedly renew the series for
season 9.

00:10:58.670 --> 00:11:02.440
By that point, however, star Zach Braff was
ready to move on to other things.

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:06.990
So the show was retooled and rebooted, no
longer taking place at a teaching hospital,

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:07.940
but at a medical school.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:11.300
Familiar doctors were now teachers instead
of active surgeons.

00:11:11.310 --> 00:11:15.029
Braff moved to the background, and the new
narrator and center of the show became Dr.

00:11:15.029 --> 00:11:15.600
Lucy Bennett.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:19.920
Ultimately, the big changes were too much
to take, and the ninth season of Scrubs was

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:20.580
its last.

